#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228862 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5bc609b-dc5b-52e9-9f0e-206f32bb829b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Homogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/id #uuid "26df399d-cd87-5b5d-9f3f-7ced4ca5fd06" :question/math-academy-id "e-21707" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-7x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) =-7y(t)$\nFind the general solution to the system of ODEs above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-7t} & te^{-7t} \\\\ 0 & e^{-7t}\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi(t) \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-7t} & te^{-7t} \\\\ 0 & e^{-7t}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= Φ_{0}(t)c \\\\\n&= e^{At}c \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-7t} & te^{-7t} \\\\ 0 & e^{-7t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}e^{-7t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}te^{-7t} \\\\ e^{-7t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{-7t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{-7t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228863 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ff837c0-0ebb-5a9e-acb4-894261e0318c" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution: Homogeneous Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "ace067cd-e1d0-5b3e-bae2-bf58dcac3585" :question/math-academy-id "e-21711" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$, where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n$\\Phi_0'(t) = A\\Phi_0(t), \\qquad \\Phi_0(0) = I_n$.\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ passing through $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ is presented below.\n\n> $\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\Phi_0(t)=e^{At}$\n> $\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t)=\\Phi_0(t)\\,\\mathbf{c}$ for some constant vector $\\mathbf{c}$\n> $\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{At}\\,\\mathbf{c}$\n> $\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=e^{A t_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}$\n> $\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $(e^{A t_0})^{-1} \\mathbf{x}(t_0)=(e^{At_0})^{-1} e^{A t_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}$\n> $\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{c} = e^{-A t_0} \\mathbf{x}(t_0) \\,$\n> $\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} e^{-At_0} \\mathbf{x}(t_0) \\,$\n> $\\textrm{L8}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At - At_0} \\mathbf{x}(t_0) \\,$\n> $\\textrm{L9}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{A(t-t_0)} \\mathbf{x}(t_0) \\,$\n\nFill in the blanks to justify each step of the following reasoning.\n\n$\\qquad$ Line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from $\\textrm{L4}$ by $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\n$\\qquad$ The property of the matrix exponential used in $\\textrm{L8}$ is $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\n$\\qquad$ Line $\\textrm{L9}$ follows from $\\textrm{L8}$ by the fact that $AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$" :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\textrm{L4}$ and $\\textrm{L5}$. Starting from $\\textrm{L4}$,\n$\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=e^{A t_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}$,\nwe multiply both sides on the left by $(e^{At_0})^{-1}$ to get\n$(e^{A t_0})^{-1}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=(e^{At_0})^{-1}e^{A t_0}\\,\\mathbf{c}$,\nwhich is line $\\textrm{L5}$. Therefore, $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from $\\textrm{L4}$ by $(e^{At_{0}})^{-1}$\n- Next, we justify the step $\\textrm{L7}$ to $\\textrm{L8}$. Since $At$ and $-At_0$ commute, we may combine exponentials:\n$e^{At}e^{-At_0}=e^{At-At_0}$.\nSubstituting this into line $\\textrm{L7}$ produces line $\\textrm{L8}$. Hence, the property of the matrix exponential used in $\\textrm{L8}$ is $X$\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L8}$ and $\\textrm{L9}$. Starting from $\\textrm{L8}$,\n$\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{At-At_0}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$,\nwe rewrite the exponent using the algebraic identity $At-At_0=A(t-t_0)$ to get\n$\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$,\nwhich is line $\\textrm{L9}$. Thus, $\\textrm{L9}$ follows from $\\textrm{L8}$ by the fact that $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt lightgray]{At-At_0=A(t-t_0)\\text{for any}t\\text{and}t_0}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228869 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"}] :question/id #uuid "b061f2b1-b015-582f-921f-9b444db631ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-21723" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 7y(t); y^{′}(t) = 7x(t)$,\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (7t) & \\sinh (7t) \\\\ \\sinh (7t) & \\cosh (7t)\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi(t) \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\mathbf{c} =e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}\n$$\n\nis the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t = t_0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = e^{At_0} \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nand since $e^{At}$ is invertible for all $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = e^{-At_0} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $\\mathbf{x}(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{At}e^{-At_{0}}x(t_{0}) \\\\\n&= e^{A(t - t_{0})}x(t_{0})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{A(t - 1)}x(1) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (7(t - 1)) & \\sinh (7(t - 1)) \\\\ \\sinh (7(t - 1)) & \\cosh (7(t - 1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sinh (7(t - 1)) \\\\ 3\\cosh (7(t - 1))\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228870 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d611c75-818f-508f-a7a4-49edb32baa41" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/id #uuid "ead811e0-8551-5b15-8b49-711cbe0b8498" :question/math-academy-id "e-21725" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 3y(t); y^{′}(t) =-3x(t)$,\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) & \\sin (3t) \\\\ -\\sin (3t) & \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}$ where $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ So, the matrix of the system is $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{x}(0)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}\n$$\n\nis the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{x}(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) & \\sin (3t) \\\\ -\\sin (3t) & \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= e^{At}x(0) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (3t) & \\sin (3t) \\\\ -\\sin (3t) & \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2\\cos (3t) - \\sin (3t) \\\\ -2\\sin (3t) - \\cos (3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5bc609b-dc5b-52e9-9f0e-206f32bb829b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Homogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e3c6cf5-b1b3-57df-abab-56efb96d89f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) \\\\ \\sin (4t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin (4t) \\\\ \\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228184} {:answer/id #uuid "a0710c2c-0243-5683-af7c-dd5379117712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) \\\\ \\sin (4t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin (4t) \\\\ \\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228185} {:answer/id #uuid "e8fa826d-432e-567c-b648-b350c15d4141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) \\\\ \\sin (4t)\\end{bmatrix}e^{4t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin (4t) \\\\ \\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228186} {:answer/id #uuid "02570066-a4ba-5ed7-9532-5cfdb98488c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) \\\\ \\sin (4t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin (4t) \\\\ \\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}e^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228187} {:answer/id #uuid "310ab06b-a4b2-54bf-bfab-dcc138d0d2d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) \\\\ \\sin (4t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sin (4t) \\\\ \\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0710c2c-0243-5683-af7c-dd5379117712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) \\\\ \\sin (4t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin (4t) \\\\ \\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228185} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e4f9160-e954-5fed-b512-345d3c9abd75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228183}] :question/id #uuid "783e954f-0aef-53d9-a6ee-3a8685affb71" :question/math-academy-id "q-334772" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-4y(t); y^{′}(t) = 4x(t)$\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (4t) & -\\sin (4t) \\\\ \\sin (4t) & \\cos (4t)\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5bc609b-dc5b-52e9-9f0e-206f32bb829b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Homogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bc6ca08-7bdb-581b-937c-a9df15057fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (3t) \\\\ \\sinh (3t)\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sinh (3t) \\\\ \\cosh (3t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228220} {:answer/id #uuid "9da73cc0-541e-5e8d-beb6-9538bcee14de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (3t) \\\\ \\sinh (3t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sinh (3t) \\\\ \\cosh (3t)\\end{bmatrix}e^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228221} {:answer/id #uuid "b63f91bf-36ee-548d-80ae-7465ec36fc8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (3t) \\\\ \\sinh (3t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sinh (3t) \\\\ \\cosh (3t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228222} {:answer/id #uuid "0db21f27-adb2-5a6d-9882-fe176b1e0a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (3t) \\\\ \\sinh (3t)\\end{bmatrix}e^{-3t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sinh (3t) \\\\ \\cosh (3t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228223} {:answer/id #uuid "6087b744-ac4b-5b75-8202-6ba65c2f600a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (3t) \\\\ \\sinh (3t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sinh (3t) \\\\ \\cosh (3t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6087b744-ac4b-5b75-8202-6ba65c2f600a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (3t) \\\\ \\sinh (3t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sinh (3t) \\\\ \\cosh (3t)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228224} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6ab42c-a905-5e2b-a54c-f11bbb07c00d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228219}] :question/id #uuid "56cd9daa-00e7-5df3-81ab-61efadc99e76" :question/math-academy-id "q-335023" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 3y(t); y^{′}(t) = 3x(t)$\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (3t) & \\sinh (3t) \\\\ \\sinh (3t) & \\cosh (3t)\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d611c75-818f-508f-a7a4-49edb32baa41" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae10838-d331-5fdb-903a-fca19877b066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2te^{-3t} \\\\ 2e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228249} {:answer/id #uuid "e7fc49fd-f1a8-52ca-bdf3-9bb225965b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2e^{-3t} \\\\ 2te^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228250} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8071ad-34b8-5a91-97ac-0395680f2065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2te^{3t} \\\\ 2e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228251} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3b14d8-5ed8-5af3-ba25-b0dba6ec8008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2te^{-3t} \\\\ 2e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228252} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5bb738-f10b-53ae-aa70-d15e698cbbc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2te^{-3t} \\\\ -2e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b3b14d8-5ed8-5af3-ba25-b0dba6ec8008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2te^{-3t} \\\\ 2e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228252} :answer-field/id #uuid "02dd2f8e-dcca-5a57-b83d-af510acff5a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228248}] :question/id #uuid "62010a37-a91d-55bc-8c8e-6361d12d6c44" :question/math-academy-id "q-335267" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-3x(t) + y(t); y^{′}(t) =-3y(t),\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{-3t} & te^{-3t} \\\\ 0 & e^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ff837c0-0ebb-5a9e-acb4-894261e0318c" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution: Homogeneous Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82f318f1-fe76-57a8-acb7-f9cc8c15543c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "using the fact that $Φ_{0}(0) = I_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228334} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5b9cc6-8a07-59ad-829f-088128beddf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "by substitution from line $L2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228335} {:answer/id #uuid "4fbdaa51-9e86-52ff-80a3-c4f13ee32da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "by substituting $t = t_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228336}] :answer-field/id #uuid "179682e5-a7b6-5acb-a487-fc439b63577f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228333} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8469de16-f6a3-5174-a08d-aa7dbfa3639a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{Mt}e^{− Mt_{0}} = e^{M(t − t_{0})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228338} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b64f10-7a08-52fc-bb2f-4bbe8e659ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$e^{X}e^{Y} = e^{X + Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228339} {:answer/id #uuid "0ea3a571-8574-50e2-b01c-014deda3a745" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{At_{0}} = e^{At}] for any matrix [MATH: A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228340} {:answer/id #uuid "606a058b-2621-563b-86d7-db0f9dbeac07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Bt − Bt_{0} = B(t − t_{0})] for any [MATH: t] and [MATH: t_{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228341}] :answer-field/id #